### Software Trigger

In addition to the hardware thresholds used to select events, jets included in these measurements must pass several cuts dcsigned to assure that each particular jet could have and should have filed the tngger Trigger simulation software at STAR takes the law BEMC ADC values measured and simulates all the tngger logic and decisions, including the masking of problematic sections of the caloumetens Using this trigger simulation, we can tell whether or not the event should have fired any of our three main triggers and, if so, which HTs and JPs were responsible

A jet coming from a HT or HTTP-triggered event must contain a HT which the simulator determined should have filed the event Similarly, a jet coming from a JP-triggered event must have a thrust axis within 6\({}^{o}\) m \(\phi\) and 0 1 in \(\eta\) of the edge of a JP that should have fired the event In the asymmetry analysis, the OR-sum of the three main triggers is used in the final result. A jet that passes any one of the three above software trigger requirements is accepted. Conversely, in the cross section analysis, these conditions are applied individually to the HT and JP samples. Further, an additional and more conservative requirement is placed on the neutral energy in the jet to require that the jet is well above the trigger thresholds. In the HT trigger we require the energy of the highest tower in the jet to be above 6.0 GeV; in the JP trigger we require the sum of the tower energies within the jet patch that fired the event to be above 8.0 GeV.

### Jet Pseudorapidity

A cone radius in \(\eta,\phi\) of 0.7 is used in this study. Calorimeter and tracking coverage at STAR are \(-0.98<\eta<2.0\) and \(-1.3<\eta<1.3\), respectively. In order to contain jets within our active \(\eta\) region, we require the jet psuedorapidity to be within \(-0.7<\eta<0.9\). This cut is asymmetric due to our asymmetric calorimeter coverage. As seen in Fig 3.2, the \(\eta\) spectrum is relatively flat within this cut window. The exception is at \(\eta\sim 0\), where there is a dip due to the jet patch locations.

Two pseudorapidity definitions for each jet are useful in this analysis. The first, \(\eta_{jet}\), is the pseudorapidity as measured through the reconstructed jet thrust axis (Eq. 3.2). The second, \(\eta_{Det}\), is the pseudorapidity value where the jet actually hits the BEMC. From the jet pseudorapidity, \(\eta_{jet}=-ln(tan(\theta_{jet}/2))\), \(\eta_{Det}\) is found by re-centering \(\theta_{jet}\) to the BEMC coordinate system. We correct \(\theta_{jet}\) with the measured \(z_{vertex}\) and BEMC radius of 231.0 cm (via \(\theta=atan(r/z)\)), and recalculate \(\eta\). The cut listed above is made to \(\eta_{Det}\). For the cross section analysis, cuts on \(\eta_{jet}\) and \(\eta_{Det}\) are both made (\(|\eta_{Det}|\), \(|\eta_{jet}|\leq 0.80\)) to be conservative.

### 4.7 Neutral Energy Fraction Cut

Particles not associated with a hard scattering but moving with the beams can interact with our detectors and reconstruct in jets. These particles originate from either the beam halo or from deflections at the focusing magnets upstream of STAR and can traverse our detectors in time with MB events registered by the BBC. Figure 4.6 (left panel) diagrams how these particles strike our detectors. The right panel gives the number of jets vs. \(\eta\) and \(\phi\) for mono-jet HT events. Lead shielding was installed between the 2005 and 2006 RHIC runs on both sides of STAR in an attempt to eliminate these background particles. The number of reconstructed background jets was reduced but not eliminated. A hole in this shielding near \(\phi\sim 0\) on the positive \(\eta\) side persisted in the 2006 data.

Due to STAR's calorimeter-based trigger, background events caused by particles traversing the calorimeters may be preferentially selected by the large amount

