Looking to the future, a polarized electron-nucleon collider is proposed[68]. An electron (or positron) beam of energy 5 to 10 GeV will collide with a nucleon beam of energy 25 to 250 GeV. Both beams will be polarized, and it is hoped that multiple collision energy scenarios are possible. With regard to the polarized nucleon spin structure, this facility has the capacity to vastly extend the kinematical reach of \(g_{1}(x,Q^{2})\) measurements. Projections show access to \(x\)-values as low as \(3\times 10^{-4}\), an unexplored phase space for polarization. With this extended reach, a global analyses of \(g_{1}\) will yield a renewed precision for the polarized PDFs.

